---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="Desktop vs Cabinet vs Travel Humidors Explained | Cigar Codex Guides"
  description="What separates a desktop humidor, a cabinet humidor, and a travel case — and which one actually fits how you smoke."
>
  <div class="home-hero">
    <div class="cigar-hero__eyebrow">Buying Guide</div>
    <h1 class="home-hero__title">Desktop vs Cabinet vs Travel Humidors Explained</h1>
  </div>

  <section class="section summary-review">
    <p>
      "Humidor" covers three genuinely different pieces of equipment, sized for three different
      collections. Buying the wrong category is the most common first-humidor mistake — not
      buying the wrong brand.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2 class="section__heading">Desktop humidors</h2>
    <p>
      A sealed box, usually cedar-lined, sitting on a shelf or desk. Typical capacity runs from
      about 20 to 100 cigars. Humidity comes from a passive humidification element inside the
      box — a foam or gel pack, a two-way humidity pouch, or a manufacturer's own system — that
      you refill or replace periodically. This is the right starting point for almost everyone:
      cheap enough to try, small enough to season and monitor easily, and sized for a collection
      you're likely to actually rotate through.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2 class="section__heading">Cabinet humidors</h2>
    <p>
      A furniture-scale piece — a standing cabinet or large chest — built for hundreds to
      thousands of cigars. These are usually electronic: a built-in humidification unit
      actively regulates RH rather than relying on a passive element, because a space this
      large can't hold humidity evenly on passive humidification alone. Cabinet humidors make
      sense once you're buying by the box regularly and need real long-term storage, not before.
      They're a meaningfully bigger investment and a bigger commitment to cigar storage as a
      hobby in its own right.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2 class="section__heading">Travel cases</h2>
    <p>
      A small, portable case — often leather, sometimes rigid plastic or metal — built to carry
      a handful of cigars (typically 2-5) safely for a day or a trip. Most don't actively
      humidify at all; they're built to protect cigars mechanically and slow moisture loss for a
      short window, not to maintain long-term RH. A few include a small humidification element
      for longer trips. A travel case isn't a replacement for a desktop humidor — it's what you
      carry cigars in after they've already been properly stored.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2 class="section__heading">Which one do you need?</h2>
    <p>
      If you're asking this question at all, you almost certainly want a desktop humidor first —
      see our <a href="/guides/best-humidors-for-beginners/">beginner humidor guide</a>. Add a
      travel case once you're regularly carrying cigars outside the house. Cabinet humidors are
      a "you'll know when you need one" purchase, not a starting point.
    </p>
    <p>
      Browse <a href="/accessories/humidors/">humidors</a> or
      <a href="/accessories/travel-cases/">travel cases</a> by Codex Score.
    </p>
  </section>
</Layout>
